{
  "dom_id": "step-q105670",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=(x-2)(x+1)^{2}.$ For which of the following values of $k$ does the intermediate value theorem **not** guarantee that $f(x)=k$ has a solution on the interval $[-2,2]?$",
  "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2],$ therefore, the intermediate value theorem guarantees that $f(x)=k$ has a solution for each value of $k$ between $f(-2)$ and $f(2).$\n\nSince $f(-2)=-4$ and $f(2)=0,$ we must have \n$k\\in [-4,0].$\n\nAmong the given options, only $k=1$ does not belong to the interval $[-4,0].$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
